# A201 — a new client per turn, and a session that outlives it (2026-09-15)

## The measurement the fix rests on
# `AUDIT/baseline/swift64/a201-probe/session-lifetime.swift`, run on node1:
$ swift AUDIT/baseline/swift64/a201-probe/session-lifetime.swift
released without invalidating -> still alive: true
released after invalidating    -> still alive: false

# So a `URLSession` that is merely released is not released, and the client that built one per turn
# left one alive per turn, each holding its delegate and its connection pool.

## The tests, against the old behaviour
# Mutation: the request builds its client inline again (as `generate` did), and `ResponseSession` has
# no `deinit`.
$ swift test --filter OpenAIResponsesClientLifetimeTests
  ✘ Test "Two turns through one engine build one client, so one session and one pool":
    Expectation failed: built.withLock { $0 } == 1
  ✘ Test "A client lets go of its session, and the session lets go of itself":
    Expectation failed: weakSession == nil
  ✘ Test "The engine's client is made once even when a turn fails":
    Expectation failed: built.withLock { $0 } == 1
  ✘ Test run with 3 tests in 1 suite failed after 0.417 seconds with 3 issues.

## With the fix
$ swift test --filter OpenAIResponsesClientLifetimeTests
  ✔ Test run with 3 tests in 1 suite passed after 0.388 seconds.

# The weak-reference case is the one worth noting: it fails without the `deinit` and passes with it, on
# the same machine seconds apart, which is what turns "the session is invalidated" from a reading of the
# code into something the suite would catch if a later edit dropped it.

